 :findings [{:id :overlapping-binder-markers
             :severity :implementation-defect
             :anchors ["parse_unparse.cljc:1810-1819" "parse_unparse.cljc:1820-1848" "parse_unparse.cljc:169-182"]
             :detail "The marker precheck validates each :- in isolation, not whether its adjacent symbol is already consumed as the previous triple's type. In [a :- b :- t/Any :-> t/Any], both markers pass the precheck. The loop first parses b as a type before reaching the leftover marker. If b cannot resolve, the result is a type-resolution error instead of named-binder/malformed; if it resolves, the later marker is diagnosed only after that parse. This violates V2's promise to diagnose malformed binder markers before type resolution. A solitary symbol after :- remains valid type syntax and must not be rejected merely for being a symbol."
             :minimal-fix "Validate marker ownership with a non-overlapping top-level grammar scan before resolving any types, without treating valid symbolic or nil types as malformed; then retain existing type resolution for a well-formed but unbound type. Add reason-specific cases for overlapping markers with an unresolvable intervening symbol, a resolvable symbolic type, incomplete/non-simple names, and a distinct well-formed unbound type diagnostic."}
            {:id :scope-frame-and-legacy-oracles
             :severity :coverage-hold
             :anchors ["parse_unparse.cljc:1416-1433" "parse_unparse.cljc:1451-1461, 1490-1506" "parse_unparse.cljc:1854-1868" "seq_annotation_test.clj:49-86"]
             :detail "Every function, including an unnamed one, pushes a frame; domain parse receives a barrier and body parsing uses a new frame. Source inspection shows the intended distinction for the reported simple cases, but candidate tests omit the required independent legacy-symbol comparison, domain-nested capture/barrier cases, standalone unnamed compatibility, explicit numeric inner and outer-offset IDs, and unbound type-variable separation. Existing negative tests check range and filter-type captures, not all domain traversal cases. parse-heterogeneous* also passes opts to nested objects and proposition sets (945-955), so scope propagation through non-function types deserves explicit cases rather than an assumption."
             :minimal-fix "Add candidate-vs-legacy comparisons with unnamed nested and standalone functions, independent symbols and literal integer warning behavior; assert a domain nested function's own binder works while an inherited named outer reference is rejected, and current not-yet-bound parameters never resolve. Cover is/! and nested heterogeneous type objects/filters, and prove no sibling leakage. Test exact preservation of literal offset IDs and their warnings. No defect in these omitted cases is claimed without execution."}
            {:id :regex-and-legacy-operator-coverage
             :severity :coverage-hold
             :anchors ["parse_unparse.cljc:1596-1712, 1716-1788, 1808-1848" "seq_annotation_test.clj:49-81" "type_rep.cljc:47-58"]
             :detail "The implementation checks r/Type? and separately rejects Regex, DottedPretype and KwArgs before counting a prefix; suffixes go to parse-cat. Existing tests cover selected optional/alt/cat/rest/dotted forms, but do not establish no-marker behavior and warning parity, context-sensitive non-final * and <... behavior, keyword tails, variable regex after a named prefix, or that each rejected form fails for its intended cause. The author also reports these gaps. The source alone does not prove the runtime classification or distribution outcomes."
             :minimal-fix "Add unchanged unnamed controls with baseline representations and exact warning captures, paired valid fixed-prefix cases before rest/dotted/keyword and supported later regex alternatives, and distinct non-final operator and nested regex rejection cases. Verify names resolve to the same fixed positional index across all generated arities; reject unsupported shapes, not silently shift them. Preserve existing deprecation warnings."}
            {:id :application-and-open-result-oracles
             :severity :coverage-hold
             :anchors ["seq_annotation_test.clj:88-105" "open_result.clj:41-76" "subst_obj.clj:21-69, 108-154" "test_utils.cljc:118-124, 126-156, 174-177"]
             :detail "The candidate uses the actual three-argument open-Result API and clj-opts; it compares returned triples and checks only the opened object id. Both caller paths are nil, so it does not test path composition or independently inspect then/else propositions. The wrong-neighbor is-tc-err merely requires some type error; an annotation/parser failure can satisfy it. There are no positional control applications, filter/Seq-path applications, or nested outer-then-inner application checks. subst-object prepends caller path, while proposition substitution concatenates latent path before caller path; identical treatment must not be assumed."
             :minimal-fix "Use actual Path constructors and distinct nonempty caller and latent paths, inspect opened object and both proposition polarities and path order against exact expected IDs/path elements as well as positional controls. Add nested two-stage opening/application, filter and Seq-path positive/negative checker examples, and use is-tc-err-messages or equivalent to assert the wrong-neighbor diagnostic rather than any error. Verify helper shapes from source before writing, and do not claim execution until authorized."}
            {:id :seq-and-test-lint-gates
             :severity :coverage-and-verification-hold
             :anchors ["typed/ann/clojure.cljc:1937-1938" "seq_annotation_test.clj:12-33, 42-47" "typed-named-binder-tests-candidate-v3.edn:9-15"]
             :detail "The seq annotation is the proposed named Seq path; the existing test compares actual resource against positional representation and preserves earlier checks, but the empty vector case has no explicit expected SeqOn result and the added matrix omits empty/no-object parity. Warning assertions are only source code, not observed results. User reports static-lint output of 16 errors and 71 warnings; this reviewer did not reproduce or classify it. Empty config and zero context do not establish the project's lint gate."
             :minimal-fix "Add typed empty and empty/no-object assertions, validate resource extraction on both baseline and candidate, and check legacy per-use integer warning separately without suppression. Compare static lint against the same baseline, configuration and environment, resolve every newly introduced error or warning, preserve historical diagnostics, and report actual project-config gate separately. No lint-pass claim from empty config."}]
